## FAQs About Fremont Lemon Law Case

## What qualifies as a "lemon" under Fremont Lemon Law?

A vehicle is deemed a “lemon” if it has a manufacturing defect. The 18-month / 18,000-mile window is a lemon-law presumption, not a requirement. It does not decide whether you have a good case. What matters is whether the defect first occurred during the original manufacturer warranty.

## Do I need a lawyer to file a Fremont Lemon Law case?

While you can file a claim without a Lemon Law Attorney Fremont, having one can be extremely beneficial. Lemon law attorneys are experienced in handling these cases and can increase the probability of earning a favorable result, especially if your case goes to court or involves complex legal issues.

## How long do I have to file a Fremont Lemon Law claim?

In California, the law of limitations for filing a lemon law claim is 4 years from the date the defect first occurred. However, it is advisable to act promptly to meet all legal deadlines. Also, it will help you get timely compensation for your claims.

## What happens if the manufacturer refuses to comply with the Lemon Law?

If the manufacturer refuses to settle your claim or offer a buyback or refund, you have to go to court. The lemon law qualifies you to seek full compensation for legal fees and other costs associated with the case if you win.

## Can I get compensation for other damages under the Lemon Law?

Yes, in addition to the buyback or refund, you may be entitled to compensation for other costs, such as towing fees, rental car expenses, and other damages caused by the faulty vehicle.
